    "body": "## What\n\nFixes #62252. `coerce_to_schema_type` tries `integer`/`number` before `string`, and Python's `int()`/`float()` accept spellings that are not valid JSON numbers: leading zeros (`0017` becomes 17), underscores (`1_000` becomes 1000), and a leading `+`. For a schema admitting both string and a numeric type (`\"type\": [\"string\", \"integer\"]` or the equivalent `anyOf`), a string value that merely looks like an integer reaches the client rewritten, and quoting does not help, so no model output can deliver the string `0017` at all. Leading zeros and similar spellings carry meaning for identifiers, ZIP codes, account numbers and version components.\n\n## How\n\nBoth numeric branches now gate on the RFC 8259 number grammar (`-?(0|[1-9]\\d*)` for integer, with the fraction/exponent extension for number) before calling `int()`/`float()`. Non-canonical spellings stay strings, including under a number-only schema (a lossy rewrite is worse than a type mismatch the client can see and reject). Canonical forms are unchanged: `\"17\"` still coerces, aliases (`int`, `long`, `uint32`, ...) still work, the whole-float-returns-int rule and the non-finite guards are untouched, and `null`/boolean/object/array/string paths are not affected.\n\nThe helper backs the qwen3_coder/qwen3_xml/mimo parsers named in the issue and every other caller (dots, k2_horizon, minimax_m3, the parser engine), so all of them pick up the fix.\n\n## Tests\n\nNew cases in `tests/tool_parsers/test_utils.py`: `0017`/`1_000`/`+17` stay strings under a mixed schema and under integer-only, `0017.5`/`1_000.5` under number, and canonical spellings still coerce. Verified locally on CPU (macOS, no GPU): the new tests fail against the unpatched helper and pass patched; the full file is 178 passed with no regressions. The repo's conftest global-cleanup fixture segfaults in this environment's torch build during teardown (pre-existing, native, unrelated to this change), so the file was also run isolated from it for a clean signal.\n",
